# Personal data and Mac safety

This rule is a **security / data-safety guardrail** at precedence tier 2 of `woodright-core.mdc`. It overrides inferred intent, broad task goals (“optimize”, “fix it”, “free memory”), and autonomous troubleshooting.

An **explicit current user instruction** may authorize only the **permission-gated** exceptions below. **Absolute** prohibitions remain non-overridable unless this rule is changed.

## Absolute vs permission-gated

**Absolute (never override via casual prompt):**

* mutate macOS system processes/services (see §2);
* `sudo`; Cursor global user settings; shell profiles; global Git config;
* Keychain / browser credential stores;
* destructive mass actions (`killall`, broad `pkill`, `rm -rf` outside approved paths);
* create / edit / load / unload / bootout of LaunchAgents or LaunchDaemons (including other labels). The **only** process exception is a kickstart of the two existing Woodright user labels named in §3 (after a failed probe).

**Permission-gated (needs exact named target in the same user message):**

* exact non-system PID / process;
* exact project Docker container + action;
* exact GUI application or URL to launch;
* exact personal file/location outside the active project.

Permission for one action does not apply to any other action.

## 1. Personal data

Do not search for, open, read, copy, index, summarize, upload, or modify personal data unless the user explicitly names the exact file or location and asks to work with it.

Protected locations and data include:

* `~/Desktop`, `~/Downloads`, and personal folders outside the active project;
* `~/Library` (except the two Woodright plist **read-only** inspects in §4);
* browser profiles, history, cookies, sessions, and saved passwords;
* Keychain data;
* email, messages, photos, contacts, calendars, and personal documents;
* SSH private keys;
* `.env` contents, passwords, API keys, tokens, certificates, and cloud credentials;
* files belonging to unrelated projects.

### Active project scope (Woodright)

Default writable/readable project work is limited to:

* the **canonical** repository `/Users/leonidmbp/Documents/projects/furniture-commerce`, or
* a **genuine Git worktree** of that repo (verify via `git worktree list`).

`/Users/leonidmbp/furniture-commerce` is a **thin mirror**, not an independent writable worktree. Follow `woodright-core.mdc` for narrowly scoped mirror sync only.

Never upload personal files, repository files, logs, or secrets to external services without explicit permission. No external network request that transmits repository or machine-derived data without explicit permission (localhost diagnostics exempt).

## 2. macOS system safety

Do not stop, restart, kill, renice, disable, unload, or modify macOS system processes and services.

This includes:

* `bird`;
* `fileproviderd`;
* iCloud processes;
* Finder;
* Dock;
* WindowServer;
* Spotlight and indexing processes;
* networking, VPN, DNS, security, login, and synchronization services.

Never use `sudo` or modify:

* macOS system settings;
* startup services **except** one `launchctl kickstart` of the existing Woodright user labels named in §3 (no plist write, no bootout/unload, no other labels);
* LaunchAgents or LaunchDaemons (create / edit / load / unload / bootout) - same kickstart-only exception;
* firewall, VPN, DNS, routing, or network interfaces;
* file permissions or ownership outside the active project;
* Cursor global user settings;
* shell profiles or global Git configuration.

## 3. Processes, Docker, and GUI applications

Without an explicit user command naming the exact target, do not:

* kill or restart any process;
* stop, restart, remove, or change Docker containers;
* run `docker update`, `docker system prune`, or destructive Docker commands;
* launch applications or URLs using `open`, AppleScript, or GUI automation;
* start persistent background services;
* use `killall`, broad `pkill`, `rm -rf`, or other mass actions.

A general request such as “fix it”, “optimize everything”, “free memory”, or “clean up the environment” is **not** permission for these actions.

### Hybrid stack restore after reboot (narrow named exception)

This exception applies only when the operator **in this message** asks to restore the local Woodright stack **after reboot** (close forms that include reboot context: «после перезагрузки», «запусти все что упало после перезагрузки»). Bare «подними стек» / «docker postgres» / «fix it» is **not** enough - those stay permission-gated.

Each action is allowed **only if its probe failed** in this turn:

1. `docker info` fails → `open -a Docker` (Docker Desktop), then bounded wait until `docker info` succeeds (do not run Compose against a dead daemon).
2. `:5432` and/or `:6379` not LISTEN → `docker compose up -d postgres redis` from the intended checkout, targeting **only** `medusa_postgres` and `medusa_redis`. Wait until Postgres is healthy before Medusa.
3. Postgres/Redis healthy **and** `:9000` not LISTEN / `/health` not 200 → **one** `launchctl kickstart` of existing `com.woodright.medusa-backend`.
4. `:3002` not LISTEN → **one** `launchctl kickstart` of existing `com.woodright.storefront-qa`. Do not kickstart storefront if it is already listening.

Still forbidden in the same restore: `docker compose --profile full`; `docker system prune`; other containers; creating/editing/loading/unloading plists; `sudo`; mass-start of `~/.woodright/durable-local-servers/*`. Ladder: `durable-local-review-servers.mdc`.

## 4. Allowed read-only diagnostics (no extra permission)

Without additional permission, the agent may run **bounded read-only** diagnostics:

* `curl` / `lsof` / `ps` / `pgrep` (including `localhost`);
* `docker ps`, `docker inspect`, and project-scoped `docker logs` (no mutating `docker exec`);
* reading **project-owned** logs under the canonical repository / genuine worktree (e.g. repo `tmp/`, `.woodright` project logs if inside scope);
* **read-only** inspect of the two existing Woodright user LaunchAgent plists (`~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.woodright.storefront-qa.plist`, `com.woodright.medusa-backend.plist`) for `WOODRIGHT_REPO_ROOT` / mode keys only. No write, copy-out of unrelated keys, or other `~/Library` paths.

Read-only process inspection is allowed. Log inspection outside the active project requires an explicitly named path. Do not display or persist env values, CLI secrets, browser data, or unrelated file paths.

Inspection must **not** change system state.

## 5. Explicit permission fallback

If the agent needs a **permission-gated** capability that is not explicitly allowed by the current instruction (access, execution rights, a file outside default project scope, a process change, a Docker mutation, a GUI action, personal data, an external path, or similar), it must stop and ask the user for permission.

**Absolute prohibitions** (§ Absolute vs permission-gated / §2) must be **refused**, not requested. Do not ask the user to authorize mutating macOS system processes, `sudo`, Keychain/browser credential access, Cursor global user settings, shell profiles, global Git config, or destructive mass actions.

The permission request must state:

* what exact action is needed;
* the exact file, path, PID, container, URL, command, or resource involved;
* why it is needed;
* what will change;
* any relevant risk.

Do not infer permission from broad phrases such as “fix it”, “continue”, “do everything necessary”, “optimize”, or “make it work”.

Silence or lack of a response is not permission.

Permission applies only to the exact action requested and does not extend to later actions.

When a potentially dangerous **permission-gated** action appears necessary:

1. stop at diagnosis;
2. explain what was found;
3. show the exact proposed action or command (as a permission request above);
4. wait for explicit user permission.

If the needed action is an **absolute prohibition**, stop at diagnosis, explain why it is forbidden, and do not propose executing it.
